{
 "lines": [
  {
   "i": 0,
   "start": 3.5,
   "end": 9.111,
   "text": "Every October, I ran the corn maze at the Marsh farm, out past the edge of town."
  },
  {
   "i": 1,
   "start": 9.711,
   "end": 13.785,
   "text": "Old Mr. Marsh gave me one rule on my first night."
  },
  {
   "i": 2,
   "start": 14.385,
   "end": 19.975,
   "text": "After the lights go off, if someone calls for help from inside the maze... don't go in."
  },
  {
   "i": 3,
   "start": 20.575,
   "end": 24.671,
   "text": "Ring the bell by the gate. And let them find their own way out."
  },
  {
   "i": 4,
   "start": 25.571,
   "end": 30.307,
   "text": "All season, the maze was empty by ten. My count always matched."
  },
  {
   "i": 5,
   "start": 31.007,
   "end": 36.553,
   "text": "Then, on the last night, a couple came running out of the corn. Their little girl was gone."
  },
  {
   "i": 6,
   "start": 37.153,
   "end": 41.612,
   "text": "Yellow raincoat. Six years old. The mother couldn't stop shaking."
  },
  {
   "i": 7,
   "start": 42.312,
   "end": 46.536,
   "text": "And deep in the rows... a tiny voice. Help me... I'm lost."
  },
  {
   "i": 8,
   "start": 48.136,
   "end": 51.101,
   "text": "I rang the bell. Again. And again."
  },
  {
   "i": 9,
   "start": 54.101,
   "end": 58.389,
   "text": "The voice stopped. The wind stopped. Even the corn went still."
  },
  {
   "i": 10,
   "start": 59.989,
   "end": 63.275,
   "text": "Please, the mother whispered. Just go get her."
  },
  {
   "i": 11,
   "start": 64.275,
   "end": 69.267,
   "text": "So I grabbed my flashlight... and checked my ticket log one last time."
  },
  {
   "i": 12,
   "start": 70.067,
   "end": 73.992,
   "text": "That couple came in at eight. Two tickets. No child."
  },
  {
   "i": 13,
   "start": 75.992,
   "end": 81.347,
   "text": "And when I looked up... they were standing at the gate, smiling. Waiting for me to go in."
  }
 ],
